  "query_preview": "..Continuing to see southwest flow over Utah and\nsouthwest Wyoming this evening between weak ridging centered near\nthe Utah/Idaho border and a closed low over the Pacific Northwest\nCoast. In this flow, moisture remains elevated over southern and\neastern Utah, with satellite derived PW values in the 0.7 to 0.9\ninch range, with a dry tongue dropping PWs into the 0.4 to 0.6\ninch range over western Utah. The elevated moisture and a bit of\nshortwave energy allowed for the development of isolated showers\nand thunderstorms over eastern Utah, including a couple of strong\nto severe cells during the early evening in Castle Country.\nTemperatures remain on the high side, with maxes having run 5-10F\nabove seasonal normals across the area. \n\nThe Pacific Northwest system is expected to be on the move tonight\ninto tomorrow, with its center approaching Montana by the early\nevening. The trough is expected to just graze the area Sunday\nevening into Monday, dropping a mostly dry cold front through.\nGuidance indicates it will push through the northwestern half of\nthe state through Monday morning before stalling and weakening.\nAhead of the front, winds will pick up further across the forecast\narea, with a decent chance of gusts to 40 mph during the afternoon\nfor western Utah as well as Uinta county, Wyoming. The lingering\nmoisture should also bring a few more showers and thunderstorms to\nsoutheast Utah, but overall instability looks a bit more limited\nthan today. \n\nFor Monday, the drop in temperatures from the front will be quite\nnoticeable over northern Utah and southwest Wyoming, with maxes\nrunning up to 10F below seasonal normals, bringing a taste of\nfall, along with further drying of the airmass. Ahead of the\nboundary, however, the decrease in temperatures is only expected\nto be a few degrees, with the deeper moisture remaining in place\nover southeast Utah, allowing for another round of showers and\nthunderstorms capable of producing heavy rain that could lead to\nisolated flash flooding. \n\nAs the Pacific Northwest trough ejects to the east by Tuesday, the\nforecast becomes a bit murkier. With the flow most likely to stay\nwesterly to southwesterly, it is looking increasingly likely there\nwill not be much change in the moisture profile through at least\nmidweek, with the deeper moisture remaining over southeast Utah as\nthe remainder of the area stays drier. With the rebuilding of high\npressure looking less likely, northern Utah and southwest Wyoming\ntemperatures are expected to rebound modestly for Tuesday, with\nmaxes staying around 5F below seasonal normals for much of the\nremainder of the week. A subset of guidance continues to show the\npossibility of a trough returning to the Great Basin for the\nlatter half of the week, with many other solutions showing a\nquieter pattern.",
